<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thyra</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">10th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Queen Thyra</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thyra was the wife of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gorm-the-old">Gorm the Old</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> and mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-bluetooth">Harald Bluetooth</a>, who lived in the tenth century. Gorm raised the smaller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jelling">Jelling</a> runestone in her memory, calling her &quot;Denmark's adornment&quot;, one of the earliest inscriptions to name Denmark. Later legend credited her with building the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danevirke">Danevirke</a> against the Germans.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thyra.jpg" alt="Thyra" width="320" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photographer Roberto Fortuna, commisioned by the Danish National Museum · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jellingsten_lille_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Thyra is one of the first queens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> whom scholars regard as historical, but no contemporary source beyond runic inscriptions survives, and her story rests on 12th- and 13th-century accounts that disagree with one another. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxo-grammaticus">Saxo Grammaticus</a> made her a daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelred-the-unready">Æthelred the Unready</a>, a claim that supported Danish kings' rights to the English throne in his own day. Sven Aggesen made her a German whose marriage was arranged by the German emperor, to whom Gorm owed tribute, and who then refused to pay it.</p><p>Gorm became king around 936, but it is unknown whether he was already married to Thyra or when she became queen. Because of her prominence on the runestones, some historians have proposed that she belonged to an older royal line and that Gorm became king by marrying her. Besides <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harald-bluetooth">Harald Bluetooth</a>, she and Gorm were probably the parents of a son, Canute; the Historia Norwegiæ adds a daughter, Gunnhild, Mother of Kings, which no other evidence supports.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The northern mound at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jelling">Jelling</a> is traditionally called Thyras Høj, though no body was found in it when it was excavated; wood from it has been dated by tree rings to 959-960, the latest date for her death if it is her grave. Saxo has her outlive Gorm, who died around 963, but she is generally believed to have died first, since he raised a stone to her. A minority view holds that the words Danmarkar bót on that stone refer to Gorm, not to her.</p><p>Saxo and Aggesen describe her as a wise queen who ordered the building of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danevirke">Danevirke</a>; Aggesen says she persuaded the emperor that the works were meant to protect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> from Gorm. The first phase of the fortifications in fact predates her, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timbers</a> felled in 737. Her name appears on five more Danish runestones. Analysis of the carving suggests that at least four, among them the Læborg and Bække 1 stones, commemorate the same woman, which would make her the person named on the most runestones of Viking Age Denmark.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thyra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Gorm raised a runestone at Jelling in memory of his wife <b>Thyra</b>, one of the earliest monuments to name the land as Denmark.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig">Duchy of Schleswig</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/denmark">Denmark</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jelling">Jelling</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/denmark">Denmark</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.17,9.25&amp;year=964" aria-label="Duchy of Schleswig on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4753 279 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Thyra"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5203 549h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5203 549h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.17,9.25&amp;year=964">What was here in 964 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
